CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 33 ----------
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Name the four compilation stages and attribute one error to each.", "Preprocessing (a missing header); compiling (a syntax or type error); assembling; linking (<code>undefined reference to ...</code>). They are separate programs, which is why each fails with its own kind of error."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "What is in an object file besides machine code?", "A symbol table — names this file defines with their offsets, and names it references but does not define — and relocations, a list of positions containing placeholders plus instructions for patching each once the real address is known."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "What are the linker's two jobs and its two failure modes?", "Symbol resolution (find a definition for every undefined symbol) and relocation (assign final addresses and patch every placeholder). It fails with undefined reference or multiple definition."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Why does link order matter in C?", "A static library is an archive of object files, and the linker pulls out only members resolving something still undefined. A library listed before the code that uses it contributes nothing."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "What are ELF's two views of the same bytes?", "Section headers — the linker's fine-grained view (<code>.text</code>, <code>.rodata</code>, <code>.data</code>, <code>.bss</code>, <code>.symtab</code>, debug info). Program headers — the loader's coarse view: a handful of segments, each a region to <code>mmap</code> with given permissions, which is the direct input to the address space layout."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Why does a program with a 100 MB zeroed global array produce a small file?", "<code>.bss</code> occupies no space in the file — only a size. The pages are allocated and zeroed at load time."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Why does <code>file</code> report a modern executable as a shared object?", "It is <code>ET_DYN</code> — position-independent, loadable at any address, which is what ASLR requires. Essentially all modern binaries are built as position-independent executables."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Map eight linker concepts onto bundler concepts.", "Object file → module; undefined symbol → import; defined symbol → export; symbol resolution → module resolution; undefined reference → Module not found; multiple definition → two copies of React; dead code elimination → tree shaking; stripping → minification; DWARF → source maps; shared library → code splitting and <code>import()</code>."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Why can ES modules be tree-shaken and CommonJS largely not?", "Dead-code elimination requires static references — <code>require(someVariable)</code> is unresolvable at build time, exactly as a function pointer defeats a linker's reachability analysis."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "What are you promising with <code>\"sideEffects\": false</code>?", "That importing a module purely for its side effects never happens, which lets the bundler drop unreferenced modules entirely — the same promise <code>--gc-sections</code> relies on."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Why does two copies of React give a hooks error rather than a duplication error?", "It is a multiple-definition problem that the JavaScript module system does not report as an error. You silently get two module instances with two separate pieces of internal state."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Explain three <code>.node</code> addon failure modes from this node.", "Architecture-specific machine code (<code>invalid ELF header</code> when built on a Mac and run in a linux/amd64 container); ABI-specific (<code>NODE_MODULE_VERSION mismatch</code>, which N-API fixes with a stable ABI); and needing a compiler at install time unless prebuilt binaries are shipped, which is what <code>prebuild-install</code> and <code>node-pre-gyp</code> do."),
("node-33", "Node 33 · Compiling and linking", "Why can a project type-check cleanly and still fail at run time with module-not-found?", "<code>tsc</code> performs the compile stages per file and there is no linker; module resolution happens at run time or bundle time, by a different tool with different rules. <code>tsc</code>'s <code>paths</code> being satisfied does not mean the runtime resolver agrees."),
# ---------- Node 34 ----------
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "What does dynamic linking defer, and who does it?", "Symbol resolution and relocation, to run time, performed by the dynamic linker named in the executable's <code>PT_INTERP</code> segment — <code>/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2</code> on Linux — before the program's first instruction."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "State dynamic linking's three benefits and three costs.", "Benefits: one copy of libc shared in memory by every process; a library security fix takes effect everywhere on next start; smaller executables. Costs: startup does work that used to be build-time; the deployment environment must supply compatible libraries; version mismatches become run-time failures."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "How do the PLT and GOT implement lazy binding?", "A call jumps to a PLT stub, which reads an address from the GOT and jumps there. Initially the GOT entry points back into the linker, so the first call triggers resolution, which writes the real address into the GOT; subsequent calls go straight through."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "What is RELRO and what does it trade?", "Resolving everything eagerly at startup and marking the GOT read-only, so an attacker cannot overwrite an entry to redirect a call. <code>LD_BIND_NOW=1</code> or <code>-z now</code>. Slightly slower startup for a closed attack surface."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "What causes <code>cannot open shared object file</code>, and how do you diagnose it?", "The library was not found on the search path (RPATH, <code>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</code>, RUNPATH, the <code>ldconfig</code> cache, defaults). <code>ldd &lt;binary&gt;</code> lists what it needs and where each was found; <code>not found</code> lines are the answer."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "What is <code>LD_PRELOAD</code> and give one good and one bad use.", "A list of libraries loaded before all others, so their symbols win — letting you replace any library function in any program with no recompilation. Good: swapping in jemalloc with one environment variable, or injecting tracing. Bad: a classic attacker persistence technique, which is why it is ignored for setuid binaries."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "State glibc's compatibility asymmetry and the rule it implies.", "Backward compatible, not forward compatible: a binary built against 2.31 runs on 2.36, but one built against 2.36 fails on 2.31 with <code>version 'GLIBC_2.34' not found</code>. So build on the oldest system you must support — which is why release pipelines pin an old base image."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "Name four real differences between Alpine and a glibc image.", "Binaries are not interchangeable, so prebuilt native modules often fall back to compiling from source; DNS resolution differs (no NSS, parallel A/AAAA on one socket) producing intermittent failures; musl's allocator is slower under multi-threaded allocation-heavy load; and the default thread stack is 128 KB rather than 8 MB."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "Give a defensible default among alpine, slim and distroless for a Node service.", "<code>node:20-slim</code> — Debian-based, glibc, ~75 MB — is the safer default. Alpine is worth it when image size genuinely matters and you have tested your dependency tree. Distroless gives glibc with no shell or package manager and the smallest attack surface, at the cost of needing ephemeral containers to debug."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "Why can a Go binary run in a <code>FROM scratch</code> image?", "It is statically linked and issues syscalls directly, so it depends on nothing but the kernel's permanent ABI. Rust with the musl target does the same. Node cannot — it is a large C++ program with a dynamic-linking-dependent addon system."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "In what sense is <code>require()</code> dynamic linking?", "It resolves a name to a file at run time, loads it, executes it, and caches it — symbol lookup, mapping, initialisation and a load cache. <code>require.cache</code> is why a module executes once, exactly as a shared object initialises once. And nested <code>node_modules</code> lets two versions coexist, which the ELF linker cannot easily do."),
("node-34", "Node 34 · Dynamic linking", "Give the two independent reasons host <code>node_modules</code> must never be copied into an image.", "The native addons were compiled for your host's architecture (Node 33) and against your host's libc (Node 34). The errors — <code>invalid ELF header</code> and a missing shared object or symbol version — do not obviously say so. <code>.dockerignore</code> containing <code>node_modules</code> is a correctness requirement, not a size optimisation."),
# ---------- Node 35 ----------
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "What does <code>execve</code> change and what does it keep?", "It replaces the program: the address space is destroyed and rebuilt. The PID, the parent, open descriptors (except close-on-exec), and credentials are kept. It creates no process."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "How does a shebang work, and who handles it?", "The kernel, not the shell: on seeing <code>#!</code> it reads the interpreter path and re-runs <code>execve</code> on the interpreter with the script as an argument. The path must be absolute, which is why <code>#!/usr/bin/env node</code> is used, and only one argument is portably allowed after it."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "Why do signal handlers reset at exec but the signal mask does not?", "The handler code no longer exists, so handlers must reset to default. The mask is inherited — a genuine trap, since a process that blocked a signal and then execs passes the block to the new program, and services have failed to receive <code>SIGTERM</code> for exactly this reason."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "When does <code>execve</code> return?", "Only on failure. On success there is nothing to return to, because the calling code no longer exists."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "What is an environment variable, precisely?", "Per-process memory: an array of <code>KEY=VALUE</code> strings copied onto the new stack at exec, with no kernel involvement afterwards."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "Derive four environment-variable behaviours from that definition.", "A child cannot modify its parent's (no channel exists); changing one after a process starts does not affect it (hence restarting a service); <code>process.env.FOO = 'x'</code> affects only children spawned afterwards; and they are visible in <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/environ</code> and <code>ps eww</code>, which is why they are a poor place for secrets."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "Why is <code>ENOENT</code> from <code>spawn</code> misleading?", "It means the <em>program</em> was not found, not your file argument. <code>PATH</code> is searched by the shell or by <code>execvp</code>, not by the bare syscall — so a minimal <code>PATH</code> produces it."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "What does a shell do with <code>ls -l | grep .js &gt; out.txt</code>?", "Tokenise and expand (globs, variables, command substitution — which is why <code>rm *.txt</code> is expanded before <code>rm</code> sees it); create a pipe; fork twice; wire descriptors with <code>dup2</code>; exec both; <code>waitpid</code>."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "What is a process group and why does Ctrl-C interrupt a whole pipeline?", "The shell puts each pipeline in its own process group and the terminal has a foreground group; Ctrl-C sends <code>SIGINT</code> to every process in it. A background process reading from the terminal gets <code>SIGTTIN</code> and stops."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "Describe <code>npm start</code>'s process tree and its three consequences.", "Shell → <code>npm</code> (a Node process) → <code>sh -c \"node server.js\"</code> → <code>node server.js</code>. Signals do not reach the bottom; exit codes are relayed badly and npm's own error output obscures the real one; and <code>node_modules/.bin</code> is prepended to <code>PATH</code>, which is the whole mechanism by which <code>\"test\": \"jest\"</code> works."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "Why does one word — <code>exec</code> — decide whether a container shuts down gracefully?", "<code>exec node server.js</code> at the end of an entrypoint script <em>replaces</em> the shell, so Node becomes PID 1 and receives signals directly. Without it the shell stays as PID 1 with Node as its child, and shutdown breaks."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "When is <code>child_process.exec</code> a security vulnerability?", "Whenever any part of the command string comes from user input: it runs through a shell, which interprets <code>;</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, backticks and <code>$()</code>. Use <code>spawn</code> with an argument array, where each argument is a separate <code>argv</code> entry and no shell is involved."),
("node-35", "Node 35 · execve", "What does <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code> do, and at which step?", "It sets <code>no_new_privs</code> so the kernel ignores setuid bits at <code>execve</code> — step one, the permission check. A near-zero-cost mitigation for a whole class of container escape."),
# ---------- Node 36 ----------
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "State the bootstrap problem and its resolution.", "To load a program you need a program. The first program is not loaded at all — it is physically present in non-volatile memory at an address the processor is wired to start from (<code>0xFFFFFFF0</code> on x86, where the chipset maps the firmware flash)."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "How does BIOS boot, and what are its two crude constraints?", "It reads the first 512-byte sector of the boot device to <code>0x7C00</code>, checks the <code>0x55AA</code> signature, and jumps. That leaves 446 bytes for code after the partition table — hence multi-stage bootloaders. And it starts the processor in 16-bit real mode, compatible with the 1978 8086."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "What does UEFI change?", "It understands FAT32 so it reads <em>files</em> from an EFI System Partition rather than raw sectors; it starts in 32- or 64-bit mode; it has a boot manager with a variable store (<code>efibootmgr</code>); and it supports Secure Boot, verifying each stage's signature — which is why an unsigned kernel module fails to load."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "What is the initramfs for?", "A chicken-and-egg fix: the kernel must mount the root filesystem, but the drivers for the controller and filesystem may live on it. So a small archive is loaded into memory as a temporary root containing exactly the modules and tools needed — storage drivers, LVM, disk encryption (the passphrase prompt) — and discarded once the real root is mounted."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "What does the kernel do between decompression and PID 1?", "Set up early page tables and switch to virtual addressing; initialise the IDT; bring secondary cores out of halt; initialise the allocator, scheduler and timers; initialise built-in drivers; mount the initramfs; find and mount the real root; and <code>exec</code> <code>/sbin/init</code>."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "What is <code>/proc/cmdline</code> and name three things you might find there.", "The kernel command line assembled by the bootloader: <code>root=UUID=...</code>, plus options like <code>transparent_hugepage=never</code>, <code>systemd.unified_cgroup_hierarchy=1</code>, or <code>mitigations=off</code>."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "Why does a container start in milliseconds?", "Nothing in this node happens — no firmware, bootloader, kernel decompression, device initialisation, initramfs or init system. It is <code>clone()</code> plus <code>execve()</code> on a kernel that is already running."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "What is cloud-init and why is the metadata endpoint a security concern?", "An early service that fetches instance metadata from <code>169.254.169.254</code>, sets the hostname, installs SSH keys, grows the root partition and runs your user-data. SSRF against that endpoint was the mechanism of the 2019 Capital One breach; IMDSv2's session-token requirement exists to make it harder."),
("node-36", "Node 36 · Boot", "Why can a container require a newer kernel feature than it will get?", "Containers do not bring a kernel. Your Alpine container on an Ubuntu host uses the Ubuntu kernel, so cgroups v2, io_uring and newer seccomp actions all depend on the <em>host</em> — which is why node image versions are worth tracking."),
# ---------- Node 37 ----------
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "What are PID 1's four jobs?", "Start services respecting dependencies; supervise them and decide about restarts with backoff; reap orphans; and shut down cleanly in dependency order with a deadline."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Name SysV init's four structural problems.", "Strictly sequential, since ordering was encoded as filename numbers; no real dependency model, only ordering; no supervision, so a crashed service stayed dead (hence daemontools, runit, supervisord, pm2); and no reliable process tracking, because a double-forking daemon detaches and PID files go stale."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Why does separating <code>Requires=</code> from <code>After=</code> matter?", "Requirement and ordering become independent, so you can require without ordering and order without requiring — and everything not explicitly ordered can start in parallel, which is where the boot-time improvement came from."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Why does systemd track services by cgroup?", "So it knows every process belonging to a service regardless of forking or daemonising — no PID files, no guessing — and <code>systemctl stop</code> can reliably kill the whole tree. It also makes <code>MemoryMax=</code>, <code>CPUQuota=</code> and <code>TasksMax=</code> available to any service."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "What is socket activation and what does it make less important?", "systemd creates the listening socket and passes the descriptor to the service when a connection arrives. Services can start on demand, and dependency ordering matters less because a client connecting to a not-yet-started service simply waits in the accept queue."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Name the systemd directives that are Part VI mechanisms, and what each is.", "<code>PrivateTmp=true</code> is a mount namespace; <code>ProtectSystem=strict</code> is read-only bind mounts; <code>MemoryMax=</code> and <code>CPUQuota=</code> are cgroup files; <code>NoNewPrivileges=true</code> is <code>no_new_privs</code>. Container mechanisms, with no container."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Map six systemd concepts to Kubernetes.", "Unit file → Deployment/Pod spec; <code>Restart=on-failure</code> → restartPolicy and CrashLoopBackOff; <code>After=</code>/<code>Requires=</code> → init containers and readiness gates; <code>MemoryMax</code>/<code>CPUQuota</code> → resources.limits (the same cgroup files); <code>TimeoutStopSec</code> → terminationGracePeriodSeconds; <code>.timer</code> → CronJob. The differences are cross-machine scheduling and declarative reconciliation."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Why is PM2 inside a container usually wrong?", "It adds a process, obscures the exit code so the orchestrator cannot see your app crashed, duplicates restart logic the orchestrator already has, and breaks signal propagation. One process per container, restart handled by the orchestrator."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Diagnose CrashLoopBackOff from an exit code.", "137: <code>SIGKILL</code>, usually OOM — check <code>lastState.terminated.reason</code>. 143: <code>SIGTERM</code>. 139: segmentation fault in a native module. 1: your application threw. 127: command not found — a wrong entrypoint path or a missing shared library. And <code>kubectl logs --previous</code> retrieves the dead container's output."),
("node-37", "Node 37 · init and systemd", "Why does Compose's <code>depends_on</code> so often need a wait script?", "It waits for the container to <em>start</em>, not to be ready — the ordering-versus-readiness distinction. <code>condition: service_healthy</code> with a <code>healthcheck</code> is the built-in fix."),
]
